Cell Biology Cells are the basic unit of life. All living organisms are made of one or more cells. 
Cells can be classified as either prokaryotic or eukaryotic. Prokaryotic cells, such as bacteria, do not have a nucleus. 
Their DNA is located in a region called the nucleoid. Prokaryotic cells are generally smaller and simpler than eukaryotic cells. 
Eukaryotic cells have a nucleus that contains their DNA. Animals, plants, fungi, and protists are made of eukaryotic cells. 
The cell membrane surrounds the cell and controls what enters and leaves the cell. It is made primarily of a phospholipid bilayer. 
The nucleus stores the cell's DNA and controls many activities of the cell. Ribosomes are responsible for making proteins. 
Mitochondria produce ATP through cellular respiration. ATP is the main form of usable energy for cells. 
The endoplasmic reticulum helps make and transport proteins and lipids. 
Rough endoplasmic reticulum has ribosomes attached to its surface, while smooth endoplasmic reticulum does not. 
The Golgi apparatus modifies, sorts, and packages proteins and other materials for transport. 
Plant cells contain structures that animal cells do not have, including a cell wall and chloroplasts. 
The cell wall provides support, while chloroplasts carry out photosynthesis. 
Vacuoles store water, nutrients, and waste. Plant cells typically have a large central vacuole.
